WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:12.996 --> 00:00:15.080
OK, I have a question for all of us.

00:00:15.104 --> 00:00:16.288
You ready?

00:00:17.050 --> 00:00:19.188
Is all winning success?

00:00:19.270 --> 00:00:20.630
(Murmurs)

00:00:20.683 --> 00:00:21.974
Oh.

00:00:22.405 --> 00:00:24.205
(Laughter)

00:00:24.229 --> 00:00:25.745
Whoa. OK.

00:00:26.541 --> 00:00:29.099
I am the recently retired head coach

00:00:29.123 --> 00:00:31.877
of the UCLA Women's Gymnastics Team,

00:00:31.901 --> 00:00:34.215
a position that I held for 29 years.

00:00:34.239 --> 00:00:35.821
(Applause)

00:00:35.845 --> 00:00:36.997
Thank you.

00:00:37.021 --> 00:00:39.225
And during my tenure,

00:00:39.249 --> 00:00:41.050
I experienced a lot of winning.

00:00:41.480 --> 00:00:44.234
I led our team to seven
National Championships,

00:00:44.258 --> 00:00:47.386
I was inducted into
the UCLA Athletic Hall of Fame

00:00:47.410 --> 00:00:50.121
and I was even voted
the Coach of the Century

00:00:50.145 --> 00:00:51.604
by the Pac-12 Conference.

00:00:51.628 --> 00:00:53.986
(Applause)

00:00:54.010 --> 00:00:57.714
Winning is really, really,

00:00:57.738 --> 00:00:59.793
like, really, really fun.

00:00:59.817 --> 00:01:02.075
(Laughter)

00:01:02.099 --> 00:01:04.635
But I am here to share my insight:

00:01:04.659 --> 00:01:06.970
winning does not always equal success.

00:01:07.756 --> 00:01:10.736
All across America and around the world,

00:01:10.760 --> 00:01:12.786
we have a crisis

00:01:12.810 --> 00:01:15.482
in the win-at-all-cost cultures

00:01:15.506 --> 00:01:17.404
that we have created.

00:01:18.272 --> 00:01:19.720
In our schools,

00:01:19.744 --> 00:01:22.462
in our businesses, in politics,

00:01:22.486 --> 00:01:25.411
winning at all cost
has become acceptable.

00:01:27.010 --> 00:01:28.907
As a society,

00:01:28.931 --> 00:01:31.663
we honor the people
at the top of the pyramid.

00:01:31.687 --> 00:01:38.350
We effusively applaud those people who win
championships and elections and awards.

00:01:39.276 --> 00:01:41.243
But sadly, quite often,

00:01:41.267 --> 00:01:44.955
those same people
are leaving their institutions

00:01:44.979 --> 00:01:47.265
as damaged human beings.

00:01:48.306 --> 00:01:50.385
Sadly, with straight A's,

00:01:50.409 --> 00:01:52.968
kids are leaving school damaged.

00:01:54.286 --> 00:01:55.627
With awards and medals,

00:01:55.651 --> 00:02:01.681
athletes often leave their teams damaged,

00:02:01.705 --> 00:02:04.080
emotionally, mentally,
not just physically.

00:02:04.993 --> 00:02:09.843
And with huge profits, employees
often leave their companies damaged.

00:02:10.812 --> 00:02:15.473
We have become so hyperfocused
on that end result,

00:02:15.497 --> 00:02:17.994
and when the end result is a win,

00:02:19.510 --> 00:02:22.400
the human component of how we got there

00:02:22.424 --> 00:02:25.643
often gets swept under the proverbial rug,

00:02:25.667 --> 00:02:27.382
and so does the damage.

00:02:28.647 --> 00:02:30.967
So I'm calling for a time-out.

00:02:30.991 --> 00:02:32.210
Time-out.

00:02:32.879 --> 00:02:34.833
We need to redefine success.

00:02:35.968 --> 00:02:42.780
Real success is developing
champions in life for our world,

00:02:42.804 --> 00:02:45.366
win or lose.

00:02:47.073 --> 00:02:51.157
(Applause)

00:02:52.105 --> 00:02:55.537
Real success is developing
champions in life,

00:02:55.561 --> 00:02:57.040
not for your team,

00:02:57.064 --> 00:02:59.431
not for your business

00:02:59.455 --> 00:03:03.423
and, I'm sad to tell you, not even
for your Christmas card bragging rights.

00:03:04.369 --> 00:03:05.558
Sorry.

00:03:05.882 --> 00:03:07.471
So how do we do this?

00:03:07.979 --> 00:03:12.460
First of all, you may be able
to dictate your way to a win,

00:03:12.484 --> 00:03:15.678
but you can't dictate your way to success.

00:03:16.781 --> 00:03:20.525
Let me take you back to 1990,
when I was first appointed the head coach

00:03:20.549 --> 00:03:22.792
of the UCLA Women's Gymnastics Team.

00:03:23.266 --> 00:03:27.172
And I would like to share with you
that I've never done gymnastics.

00:03:27.196 --> 00:03:29.172
I grew up in the world of ballet.

00:03:29.196 --> 00:03:32.038
I have never done a cartwheel,

00:03:32.062 --> 00:03:34.953
and I couldn't teach you
how to do a proper cartwheel.

00:03:34.977 --> 00:03:36.022
(Laughter)

00:03:36.046 --> 00:03:37.335
It's sadly true.

00:03:37.359 --> 00:03:42.617
And I knew nothing about
how to develop a team culture.

00:03:43.283 --> 00:03:47.025
The best I could do was mimic
other coaches who had won.

00:03:47.728 --> 00:03:49.810
And so I became tough-talking,

00:03:49.834 --> 00:03:52.275
tough-minded, relentless,

00:03:53.219 --> 00:03:54.914
unsympathetic,

00:03:54.938 --> 00:03:57.241
bullish, unempathetic

00:03:58.487 --> 00:04:00.827
and oftentimes downright mean.

00:04:02.345 --> 00:04:05.646
I acted like a head coach

00:04:05.670 --> 00:04:08.948
whose only thought
was to figure out how to win.

00:04:11.553 --> 00:04:13.421
My first few seasons as a head coach

00:04:13.445 --> 00:04:14.870
were abysmal,

00:04:14.894 --> 00:04:18.852
and after putting up with
my brash coaching style for a few years,

00:04:18.876 --> 00:04:21.224
our team asked me for a team meeting.

00:04:21.248 --> 00:04:22.565
Well, I love team meetings,

00:04:22.589 --> 00:04:24.723
so I said, "Yay!
Let's have a team meeting."

00:04:25.352 --> 00:04:27.865
And for two solid hours,

00:04:27.889 --> 00:04:33.650
they gave me examples of how my arrogance
was hurtful and demeaning.

00:04:34.603 --> 00:04:35.852
Yeah, not yay.

00:04:37.501 --> 00:04:39.176
They explained to me

00:04:39.200 --> 00:04:41.292
that they wanted to be supported,

00:04:41.316 --> 00:04:42.744
not belittled.

00:04:42.768 --> 00:04:45.377
They wanted to be
coached up, not torn down.

00:04:45.401 --> 00:04:47.983
They wanted to be motivated,

00:04:48.007 --> 00:04:50.313
not pressured or bullied.

00:04:51.476 --> 00:04:53.235
That was my time-out,

00:04:54.242 --> 00:04:55.937
and I chose to change.

00:04:57.689 --> 00:05:00.948
Being a dogmatic dictator

00:05:00.972 --> 00:05:04.525
may produce compliant,
good little soldiers,

00:05:04.549 --> 00:05:07.363
but it doesn't develop champions in life.

00:05:08.278 --> 00:05:12.443
It is so much easier, in any walk of life,

00:05:12.467 --> 00:05:14.928
to dictate and give orders

00:05:14.952 --> 00:05:18.113
than to actually figure out
how to motivate someone

00:05:19.015 --> 00:05:20.784
to want to be better.

00:05:20.808 --> 00:05:23.024
And the reason is -- we all know this --

00:05:23.048 --> 00:05:25.727
motivation takes a really long time

00:05:25.751 --> 00:05:27.111
to take root.

00:05:27.592 --> 00:05:29.814
But when it does,

00:05:29.838 --> 00:05:31.869
it is character-building

00:05:31.893 --> 00:05:33.679
and life-altering.

00:05:34.464 --> 00:05:39.127
I realized that I needed
to fortify our student-athletes

00:05:39.151 --> 00:05:41.165
as whole human beings,

00:05:42.119 --> 00:05:43.755
not just athletes who won.

00:05:44.355 --> 00:05:46.911
So success for me shifted

00:05:46.935 --> 00:05:49.483
from only focusing on winning

00:05:49.507 --> 00:05:52.194
to developing my coaching philosophy,

00:05:52.218 --> 00:05:56.737
which is developing champions
in life through sport.

00:05:57.869 --> 00:05:59.862
And I knew if I did this well enough,

00:05:59.886 --> 00:06:03.277
that champion mentality would translate
to the competition floor.

00:06:04.230 --> 00:06:05.456
And it did.

00:06:06.343 --> 00:06:09.479
The key ingredient was to develop trust

00:06:10.439 --> 00:06:11.859
through patience,

00:06:12.515 --> 00:06:14.490
respectful honesty

00:06:15.555 --> 00:06:17.053
and accountability --

00:06:18.186 --> 00:06:21.121
all of the ingredients
that go into tough love.

00:06:22.820 --> 00:06:24.753
Speaking of tough love,

00:06:24.777 --> 00:06:27.654
Katelyn Ohashi is
a perfect example of this.

00:06:27.678 --> 00:06:30.229
You may have all seen her floor routine.

00:06:30.253 --> 00:06:34.317
It has had over 150 million views.

00:06:34.785 --> 00:06:38.729
And the consensus is,
her performance is pure joy.

00:06:39.434 --> 00:06:44.696
However, when Katelyn came to UCLA,
she was broken in body, mind and spirit.

00:06:45.691 --> 00:06:51.130
She had grown up in a stereotypical,
very high-level athletic world,

00:06:52.054 --> 00:06:53.618
and she was damaged.

00:06:54.641 --> 00:06:57.691
So when Katelyn came
to UCLA her freshman year,

00:06:57.715 --> 00:07:01.093
she found her inner rebel quite well,

00:07:02.085 --> 00:07:04.909
to the point where she was
no longer able to do gymnastics

00:07:04.933 --> 00:07:06.865
at the level at which she was recruited.

00:07:07.427 --> 00:07:09.518
And I will never forget

00:07:09.542 --> 00:07:12.809
a team meeting we had
halfway through her freshman season.

00:07:12.833 --> 00:07:16.146
We were in there with the team,
the coaching staff, the support staff,

00:07:16.170 --> 00:07:17.701
sports psychologist,

00:07:17.725 --> 00:07:23.166
and Katelyn very clearly
and unapologetically said,

00:07:24.212 --> 00:07:26.214
"I just don't want to be great again."

00:07:28.520 --> 00:07:30.379
I felt like I got sucker punched.

00:07:31.813 --> 00:07:34.194
My first thought was,

00:07:34.218 --> 00:07:37.726
"Then why the heck am I
going to honor your scholarship?"

00:07:39.390 --> 00:07:42.861
It was a really snarky thought,
and thankfully I didn't say it out loud,

00:07:42.885 --> 00:07:45.895
because then I had clarity.

00:07:46.450 --> 00:07:48.649
Katelyn didn't hate gymnastics.

00:07:49.355 --> 00:07:52.790
Katelyn hated everything
associated with being great.

00:07:53.485 --> 00:07:55.911
Katelyn didn't want to be a winner,

00:07:55.935 --> 00:08:00.377
because winning at all cost
had cost her her joy.

00:08:01.795 --> 00:08:05.829
My job was to figure out
how to motivate her

00:08:05.853 --> 00:08:07.682
to want to be great again,

00:08:08.740 --> 00:08:11.839
by helping her redefine success.

00:08:14.601 --> 00:08:18.467
My enthusiasm for that challenge
turned into determination

00:08:18.491 --> 00:08:21.534
when one day Katelyn
looked me in the eye and said,

00:08:21.558 --> 00:08:23.879
"Ms. Val, I just want you to know,

00:08:23.903 --> 00:08:26.871
everything you tell me to do,
I do the exact opposite."

00:08:26.895 --> 00:08:29.559
(Laughter)

00:08:29.583 --> 00:08:32.793
Yeah, it was like, yeah, Katelyn,
challenge accepted. OK.

00:08:32.817 --> 00:08:34.340
(Laughter)

00:08:34.985 --> 00:08:38.596
And further proof that dictating
was not going to win.

00:08:39.111 --> 00:08:42.378
So I embarked on
the painfully slow process

00:08:42.402 --> 00:08:44.123
of building trust

00:08:44.147 --> 00:08:46.744
and proving to her that first and foremost

00:08:46.768 --> 00:08:50.272
I cared about her as a whole human being.

00:08:52.931 --> 00:08:56.849
Part of my strategy was to only talk
to Katelyn about gymnastics in the gym.

00:08:57.955 --> 00:09:00.435
Outside of the gym,
we talked about everything else:

00:09:00.459 --> 00:09:02.984
school, boys, families,
friends, hobbies.

00:09:03.008 --> 00:09:07.385
I encouraged her to find things
outside of her sport that brought her joy.

00:09:08.197 --> 00:09:10.986
And it was so cool

00:09:11.010 --> 00:09:17.483
to see the process of Katelyn Ohashi
literally blossom before our eyes.

00:09:17.507 --> 00:09:19.530
And through that process,

00:09:20.355 --> 00:09:23.586
she rediscovered her self-love

00:09:23.610 --> 00:09:25.112
and self-worth.

00:09:26.702 --> 00:09:30.632
And slowly, she was able to bring that joy

00:09:31.425 --> 00:09:33.330
back to her gymnastics.

00:09:34.506 --> 00:09:38.829
She went on to earn
the NCAA title on floor,

00:09:38.853 --> 00:09:43.355
and she helped our team win
our seventh NCAA championship in 2018.

00:09:44.675 --> 00:09:45.665
So --

00:09:45.805 --> 00:09:46.747
Thank you.

00:09:46.765 --> 00:09:48.938
(Applause)

00:09:50.679 --> 00:09:54.312
So let's think about
the Katelyn Ohashis in your life.

00:09:55.294 --> 00:09:58.653
Let's think about those people
under your care and your guidance.

00:10:00.124 --> 00:10:03.364
What are you telling your kids
on the car ride home?

00:10:05.431 --> 00:10:06.788
That car ride home

00:10:06.812 --> 00:10:09.878
has much more impact than you know.

00:10:12.182 --> 00:10:14.094
Are you focusing on the end result,

00:10:14.929 --> 00:10:16.641
or are you excited to use that time

00:10:16.665 --> 00:10:18.959
to help your child
develop into a champion?

00:10:19.771 --> 00:10:20.986
It's very simple:

00:10:21.010 --> 00:10:23.362
you will know you're focusing
on the end result

00:10:23.386 --> 00:10:25.757
if you ask questions about the end result.

00:10:26.234 --> 00:10:27.431
"Did you win?"

00:10:28.140 --> 00:10:29.693
"How many points did you score?"

00:10:30.404 --> 00:10:31.795
"Did you get an A?"

00:10:33.890 --> 00:10:39.610
If you truly are motivated about helping
your child develop into a champion,

00:10:39.634 --> 00:10:42.706
you will ask questions
about the experience

00:10:42.730 --> 00:10:44.047
and the process,

00:10:44.071 --> 00:10:46.421
like, "What did you learn today?"

00:10:47.072 --> 00:10:48.570
"Did you help a teammate?"

00:10:49.523 --> 00:10:51.509
And, my favorite question,

00:10:51.533 --> 00:10:54.935
"Did you figure out how to have fun
at working really, really hard?"

00:10:57.021 --> 00:11:00.587
And then the key is to be very still

00:11:00.611 --> 00:11:02.734
and listen to their response.

00:11:04.501 --> 00:11:09.085
I believe that one of the greatest gifts
we can give another human being

00:11:09.109 --> 00:11:11.590
is to silence our minds

00:11:11.614 --> 00:11:14.124
from the need to be right

00:11:14.148 --> 00:11:17.145
or the need to formulate
the appropriate response

00:11:17.169 --> 00:11:18.740
and truly listen

00:11:18.764 --> 00:11:20.556
when someone else is talking.

00:11:21.518 --> 00:11:24.347
And in silencing our minds,

00:11:24.371 --> 00:11:28.658
we actually hear our own fears
and inadequacies,

00:11:28.682 --> 00:11:32.156
which can help us formulate our response

00:11:32.180 --> 00:11:35.398
with more clarity and empathy.

00:11:38.216 --> 00:11:40.106
Kyla Ross, another one of our gymnasts,

00:11:40.130 --> 00:11:42.968
is one of the greatest gymnasts
in the history of the sport.

00:11:42.992 --> 00:11:45.805
She's the only athlete
to have earned the trifecta:

00:11:46.362 --> 00:11:48.361
she's a national champion,

00:11:48.385 --> 00:11:49.978
a world champion

00:11:50.002 --> 00:11:51.526
and an Olympic champion.

00:11:52.465 --> 00:11:54.839
She's also not one for small talk,

00:11:54.863 --> 00:11:57.732
so I was a bit surprised one day
when she came to my office,

00:11:57.756 --> 00:11:59.081
sat on the couch

00:11:59.105 --> 00:12:01.421
and just started talking --

00:12:01.445 --> 00:12:03.143
first about her major,

00:12:03.167 --> 00:12:04.732
then about graduate school

00:12:04.756 --> 00:12:08.536
and then about everything else
that seemed to pop into her mind.

00:12:09.186 --> 00:12:12.561
My inner voice whispered to me

00:12:12.585 --> 00:12:14.644
that something was on her mind,

00:12:14.668 --> 00:12:16.718
and if I was still

00:12:16.742 --> 00:12:19.168
and gave her enough time,

00:12:19.192 --> 00:12:20.509
it would come out.

00:12:22.097 --> 00:12:23.282
And it did.

00:12:24.885 --> 00:12:28.096
It was the first time that Kyla
had shared with anyone

00:12:29.739 --> 00:12:32.817
that she had been
sexually abused by Larry Nassar,

00:12:34.095 --> 00:12:36.524
the former USA Gymnastics team doctor,

00:12:36.548 --> 00:12:40.675
who was later convicted
of being a serial child molester.

00:12:43.755 --> 00:12:45.791
Kyla came forward

00:12:45.815 --> 00:12:47.696
and joined the army

00:12:47.720 --> 00:12:49.402
of Nassar survivors

00:12:50.436 --> 00:12:52.665
who shared their stories

00:12:52.689 --> 00:12:54.227
and used their voices

00:12:55.194 --> 00:12:58.904
to invoke positive change for our world.

00:13:02.073 --> 00:13:05.034
I felt it was extremely
important at that time

00:13:05.058 --> 00:13:07.978
to provide a safe space
for Kyla and our team.

00:13:08.927 --> 00:13:12.671
And so I chose to talk about this
in a few team meetings.

00:13:15.896 --> 00:13:18.696
Later that year, we won
the national championship,

00:13:18.720 --> 00:13:21.935
and after we did, Kyla came up to me
and shared with me the fact

00:13:21.959 --> 00:13:24.821
that she felt one reason that we'd won

00:13:24.845 --> 00:13:27.514
was because we had addressed
the elephant in the room,

00:13:28.459 --> 00:13:32.159
the tragedy that had
not only rocked the world

00:13:32.183 --> 00:13:37.064
but that had liberated the truths
and the memories in herself

00:13:37.088 --> 00:13:39.310
and in so many of her friends

00:13:39.334 --> 00:13:41.517
and her peers.

00:13:43.019 --> 00:13:44.746
As Kyla said,

00:13:44.770 --> 00:13:48.460
"Ms. Val, I literally felt myself
walk taller as the season went on,

00:13:49.606 --> 00:13:53.135
and when I walked onto that
championship floor, I felt invincible."

00:13:53.397 --> 00:13:54.547
Simply --

00:14:01.158 --> 00:14:08.158
(Applause)

00:14:13.111 --> 00:14:15.217
Simply because she had been heard.

00:14:17.931 --> 00:14:21.139
As parents, as coaches,

00:14:21.163 --> 00:14:22.925
as leaders,

00:14:22.949 --> 00:14:25.610
we can no longer lead from a place

00:14:27.183 --> 00:14:31.471
where winning is
our only metric of success,

00:14:31.495 --> 00:14:34.624
where our ego sits center stage,

00:14:34.648 --> 00:14:36.251
because it has been proven

00:14:36.275 --> 00:14:39.630
that that process produces
broken human beings.

00:14:40.424 --> 00:14:42.779
And I emphatically know

00:14:42.803 --> 00:14:45.362
that it is absolutely possible

00:14:45.386 --> 00:14:47.666
to produce and train champions in life

00:14:47.690 --> 00:14:50.737
in every single walk of life

00:14:50.761 --> 00:14:54.147
without compromising the human spirit.

00:14:55.904 --> 00:15:00.385
(Applause)

00:15:02.975 --> 00:15:06.435
It starts with defining success

00:15:06.459 --> 00:15:09.806
for yourself and those under your care

00:15:11.081 --> 00:15:13.622
and then consistently

00:15:13.646 --> 00:15:18.759
self-examining whether your actions
are in alignment with your goals.

00:15:20.791 --> 00:15:23.622
We are all coaches in some capacity.

00:15:24.987 --> 00:15:28.777
We all have a collective responsibility

00:15:28.801 --> 00:15:33.043
to develop champions
in life for our world.

00:15:33.716 --> 00:15:36.682
That is what real success looks like,

00:15:37.587 --> 00:15:39.318
and in the world of athletics,

00:15:40.233 --> 00:15:42.593
that is what we call a win-win.

00:15:43.286 --> 00:15:44.451
Thank you.

00:15:44.475 --> 00:15:47.211
(Applause)

